From 956d4a2ec74b53b365403c98b93fbd405a295aeb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 14:12:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] media: iris: ROG5: safe publication, reload and PM
 reference fixes

From the k116 RAM trials (module reload reset the phone) and the
GPT-6.1-Sol audit 16 of the video driver:

- The video nodes were registered before video_set_drvdata(), and
  driver data, DMA mask and runtime PM were set up after them. udev's
  v4l_id opens a new node at once; iris_open() then dereferenced a NULL
  core. That is a concrete candidate for the reset on re-probe. Set the
  node data before registering it and publish the nodes last.
- While authenticated firmware runs, the module is pinned (from
  iris_fw_load() until the PAS shutdown and the power off after it have
  completed without a hold; a held core stays pinned, and a teardown that
  ends held now leaves the core in IRIS_CORE_ERROR instead of DEINIT):
  `modprobe -r qcom_iris` is refused instead of releasing power domains,
  votes and mappings under a running firmware. New write-only device
  attribute firmware_unload shuts an idle core down (refused while an
  instance exists or an open is in progress); a later open loads the
  firmware again.
- Runtime suspend is refused while the core is still powered after a
  fatal error the containment work has not handled yet (it returned
  success and dropped the MX/MMCX supplier references).
- iris_scale_power() dropped its runtime PM reference before updating
  clocks and bandwidth; it now holds it across both updates. Decoder
  QBUF no longer queues work after a failed vote.
- Markers at probe end and remove.

ROG5: patch 0169, kernel k117. Not in upstream.
